Subject: [PATCH 4/4] WP-0.7, enforcement (cld-advisor's patch 4, built on
 ea41e6fc9): A COMMIT DATED BEFORE ITS PARENT IS NOT TRUSTED FOR ITS DAY (the
 seat's own variant of PV15; no planted case of the suite). With a gate's day
 taken from the commit, whoever makes the commit can move it: dated a month
 back, a commit's debts are a month younger. After ea41e6fc9 no commit on the
 first-parent line may be dated, by its committer time, before its parent; the
 rule reads every commit after its root and not the tip alone, so a later
 commit dated rightly does not hide an earlier one, and it fails closed where
 the history cannot be read back to its root. The four debt gates run their
 checkers through one helper that asks this first, and are refused on a day
 that cannot be trusted. Read on main at this commit: none of its 2,985
 first-parent commits is dated before its parent, so nothing is frozen.
 relay-debt and point-debt now read the past and move in the table. WHAT THE
 RULE DOES NOT BOUND: between its parent's date and the real time a commit's
 date is its maker's to choose; a gate cannot know the real time without a
 clock. THE WAY OUT if a mis-dated commit is ever on main: history is not
 rewritten, so the rule's first commit is moved past it in a commit that says
 why, and the lock pins that commit so the move shows in two places. The lock:
 two tests added to tests/test_gates_are_a_function_of_the_commit.py. Main may
 drop this patch and keep the three before it. 0 of 19.

